 your  title can be really helpful a  description is one paragraph that's  focused on the value of your package so  here's the Broom package i mentioned  earlier and i really like its  description because it does a couple  things it starts with a summary sentence  so this is about we see summarizes key  information about statistical objects  and tidy Tibbals it then goes into the  benefits why is this helpful  well it makes it easy to report results  or work with a large number of models at  once and in brooms case it has three  main functions and so it makes sense in  the description to describe those three  functions and talk about what it does a  readme is a is a nice little bit longer  form documentation but ideally not too  long and it's a great place to point  people where to start and where to go  next you know many people might only see  this and not read the longer form  documentation I talked about with the  vignette so to read me a good place to  start as an overview this is the for  cats readme which I helped contribute to  in the tidy verse developer day and it  starts out with what's the goal of for  cats well it's make working with factors  easier I show some of the functions that  the forecast package has and why you  might want to use them and I also point  people to where they can learn more next  I give some installation instructions  and this is helpful as well to know if  the packages on cran if you can use  install that packages and then give some  examples so in the readme case you don't  want to necessarily overwhelm people but  it's helpful to give you know one or two  examples of how a person might use this  so in this case I give an example of how  FCT underscore lump can take a factor  with lots of levels and put the ones  that aren't very frequent into one  factor level called other a vignette is  this longer form it's a case study it's  I advise to use real and interesting  data for this so don't make something  like a B and C and like very artificial  for how you would use your package it's  really nice to give people an example of  how could they use this in the real  world if it's  statistics package you can use it make a  model if it's a visualization package  make a graph so it really makes sense a  tailor to what the goal of your package  is so in this case with for cats because  for cats is working with factors some  functions they are helpful with graphs  so in this case I show how a for cats  function can help reorder your graph  into the ascending order  I also repeat a little bit of the readme  and show with FCT lump so it's okay here  to be repetitive it's okay to have some  shared things across your documentation  because again people might not see them  all well now we have a pretty good  package and it's nicely documented how  can you improve it and there of course  lots of ways but I want to concentrate  on one so who's had this before where  something's working it's going great  everything's fine and then right where  you need it it just completely falls out  from under you so how can you prevent  these cases solution here is unit test  so this is a nice quote from having  Wickham which says you may think I am  making these silly mistakes on purpose  for teaching purposes that is not true I  make these mistakes all the time and  it's the unit test that saves me so it  doesn't matter how good an our  programmer you are you will eventually  make some mistakes or when you're  refactoring your package something that  worked before will break and so unit  tests are how you can keep developing  safely how you can write tests you first  set up with use this use underscore test  that and just like use rock to Jen MD  this one you only have to do once and  this sets up the structure and then you  make a file use test for I Love New York  our conference so in this case I made  two tests I wanted to test that the  function defaults to true and that it  fails if it's given not a logical so  test that works you do things like  expect underscore equal in this case  expect underscore error I expect this to  throw an error I expect this to be true  I expect this to be greater than so you  write down your expectations and then  you can have it so that you check when  you're developing new functions you can  always be checking that it's passing  your tests so I really recommend to  write them early and often because that  way you can safely iterate knowing that  your core functionality is not affected  and whenever you fix a bug put in a test  would have caught that because again  that's an error you've already seen  happen and you know just because it's  happened once it absolutely can happen  again even if you fix it so right  something that would catch that and let  you know if it's reintroduced so a final  thing is how do you market your package  and I'd like to start by saying  marketing is not a bad word I think it  could have a negative connotation but  it's equally important even if it's  something just used within your company  or team because marketing will help  people know what they can get out of  your package and it helps them it's not  a selfish thing you've written this  because it's useful for you and that  makes it very likely that it's useful  for other people so the documentation  was a great start a vignette or readme  will all help explain the value but  there are a couple ways you can go  beyond that the first is to publish your  package on github if you're not familiar  with git or github it's beyond the scope  of this talk but I highly recommend  reading Jenny Brian's book happy git and  github for the use are because it walk  you through step by step how to get  started and this is a way that really  lets you easily share your package with  other people and also eventually maybe  have other people contribute to it too  because github allows folks to file  issues if something is not working for  them a pull request which says hey I've  written some code that maybe as an  additional test or fixes a bug and I  think can help develop your package so  it's a nice way to integrate to have the  community help with your package  development as well make a website with  package down so I actually just made one  for a package that I wrote just a few  days ago and I really liked how easy it  was so you use the package down function  build site you push it to github you  change one little setting and voila you  have a whole package and what this does  is package down takes the readme you've  written the vignette the details in your  descriptions we see the developers here  on the right and it takes all of that  what you've already written and just  repurposes that into a website so once  you've done that documentation it's  really easy to them make a website and  share that with more people you can blog  and tweet about it this is a night suite  from Jim Hester about his package room I  recommend if you do this use the are  stats hash tag it's a really great way  to have folks even if they don't follow  you to find out and discover  your package give the a one line  motivation what again what problem would  this help users saw solve why would they  care so in this case it's to read CSV  TSV and fix with files quickly you can  remit remix your readme and vignette in  this blog post so again there's a  there's a common you know idea of dry  don't repeat yourself in programming and  I actually say with documentation  repeating yourself is good again maybe  some people will find your blog some  people find the readme some people will  find the package down site some people  will see the vignette it's okay to have  one core message across all of these  things and to be reusing some stuff  again tailored for that format the final  thing is you can submit your package to  crayon and this could be a whole talk in  itself but I will just say with Hadleys  quote here that the majority of our  users do not install packages from  github so github is a great way to start  sharing a package but if you want it to  be really widely used it will have to go  to cran that being said I think it's  nice to wait to submit to cran until you  have a stable user facing API you don't  want to go ahead and submit if you're  going to be changing a ton of things  that will mean the functionality users  have come to depend on will change  finally if you're successful you can see  this is my brother dear off here about  his broom package how he felt when he  first got his first github pull request  I was really exciting the community was  engaging  Mickey's happy how he feels now when he  looks at the github issues for broom  fortunately broom has been taken over by  another maintainer maybe for this reason  so it is a really exciting thing you  know and eventually it can become you  know bigger than you ever imagined so I  like to end this talk which is a few  resources first can I get a bit of a  drumroll please  there is a new are packaged book in the  works so the first are packaged book  from Hadley Wickham a couple years ago  excellent resource but didn't have some  of the newer tools like use this  jenny/brian is now writing a new edition  with Hadley it's still in development  but you can find it at our - packages  org it's already excellent it's again  it's still in the works but has some  really great new material and from the  advanced are some other resources that  are really helpful I won't go through  them all but these are some great blog  posts and tutorials on different parts  of writing a  [ __ ] and with that thank you all so  much and look forward to speaking to you  for the rest of the conference  [Applause]
 

